WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.379 --> 00:00:14.320
and to move on to item four on the Agenda,
which is the presentation by Jane on transforming

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.040
care for people with learning disabilities.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.289
Jane Cummings Chief Nursing Officer and NHS
Learning Disability Programme SRO: I have

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:22.609
a few key messages that it is worth sharing
with the Board.

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:27.909
The first one is that we are seeing a reduction
in the number of inpatients that we have at

00:00:27.909 --> 00:00:29.579
the moment, which is positive.

00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:33.270
We are seeing a reduction in the number of
admissions and an increase in the number of

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.370
discharges, which is in the right direction.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:41.360
Of the patients who were inpatients in April
2014, 30% of those have been discharged and

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.600
a further 13% have been moved to more appropriate
inpatient settings, so we are seeing that

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.300
significant shift.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.460
The Board will know and I have talked previously
about the Care and Treatment Reviews that

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.100
we established last year.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.079
We have now done over 1,400 of those.

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:03.080
What has been interesting is not only has
that prompted and encouraged discharges, but

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:09.860
it has also identified a number of key issues
that we are tackling on a case-by-case basis.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:17.750
As an example, for patients who we thought
could be discharged but were not, gaining

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:22.170
agreement on a suitable plan was one of the
key reasons.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:28.400
That was based on having clinicians agreeing
with families, with our reviewers, that it

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.670
was appropriate for people to be discharged
and into what type of service.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:38.609
We also found that there was a lack of community
support, so the right provider or the right

00:01:38.609 --> 00:01:44.229
staff in the community to be able to support
people out of hospital.

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:49.279
Some patients were subject to Ministry of
Justice orders and restrictions, and that

00:01:49.279 --> 00:01:52.820
in itself meant that there were some delays
in being able to discharge people.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:59.409
All of that has given us a significant amount
of information in order to support what we

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:02.549
are doing, which is developing new models
of care.

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:07.420
We are currently working on a new commissioning
framework which will look at new standards

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.140
and new models.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:14.910
We have a plan and we will deliver that new
model in July.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:18.020
Alongside that, we are identifying fast track
communities.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:22.750
In a similar way to the vanguards that we
have for the new models of care for the Five

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:28.830
Year Forward View, we are going to have five
fast track communities which are based around

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.850
the areas that have the biggest inpatients,
that is, around the hospitals, where about

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.450
29% of the beds are currently situated.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.790
With those fast tracks, which will be health
and social care combined, we are expecting

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:46.720
there to be transformation plans developed
over the summer, submitted to us in September

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.340
and, alongside that, developing an improvement
programme similar to the one that we have

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.940
for the vanguards, which I know Ian will talk
about subsequently.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:03:00.980
That will give us the collaborative learning,
the best practice and the lessons that we

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:07.720
can then use to influence and implement in
the 2016/17 planning round.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.670
The other key message I want to be absolutely
clear about is that we have committed publicly

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:18.890
and I reiterate that by autumn we will have
closure and reconfiguration plans around those

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.570
regions where large providers are based.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:27.260
We will then be able to implement some of
those plans for the latter part of this year

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.950
and into next.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.830
I also wanted to point out the work we are
doing around stakeholder engagement.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.620
It is absolutely critical.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:40.850
This is a really good system where we need
to do this in co production and work alongside

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:42.290
key stakeholders.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:45.560
We have established a reference group, which
is chaired by Professor Tony Holland, who

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.580
is a psychiatrist and an academic from Cambridge
University.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:54.910
He and Scott Watkins, who is a person with
learning disabilities, are co chairing that

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:56.790
reference group.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:02.850
Supporting that reference group, we are creating
fora: one for commissioners, one for providers,

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.360
one for patients and carers and one for clinicians.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:13.560
They will help co design and engage with us
as we develop that work.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:19.079
We have an inaugural Learning Disability Forum
meeting, which is being established on 16th

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:20.079
June.

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:23.540
The letters for that are going out today.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:29.290
That sits alongside the LD Awareness Week,
where we have an event both in London and

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:34.980
in Leeds, which will be for our stakeholders
and also for NHS England staff.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:42.100
The plan is that we work with and have a marketplace
with people like Mencap and the Challenging

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:47.650
Behaviour Foundation, as well as carers and
patients, to help to establish and tell the

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:51.630
story to all our staff about why we are doing
this work, not just to those who are involved

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:52.630
in the programme.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:59.770
I am also pleased to confirm that we have
now employed a couple of people with a learning

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:01.350
disability.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:07.790
One of those is supporting the Patient Engagement
Team and the other one is supporting the Care

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:11.580
and Treatment Reviews and Improving Lives
Team.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.180
Hence we have done what we said, which is
that we would directly employ people with

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:17.860
a learning disability in NHS England.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:23.520
I have to say, it has not been straightforward
when you start to do that.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:29.020
Being able to use things like NHS Jobs to
be able to design both job descriptions and

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:35.889
application forms in Easyread and being able
to organise how those jobs are graded and

00:05:35.889 --> 00:05:41.120
banded is difficult, because it is not the
same principle that we would usually use.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:44.930
Having done that, we have learned quite a
lot of lessons, and those lessons we are using

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:52.100
to support the work that Ian and his team
are doing around wider employment across health

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.420
and other sectors.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:57.530
That has been a very quick summary of where
we are and the key messages.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:02.810
This is a very complex programme, but it is
one that we have a good grip on.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:06.970
We know that the NHS is a complex organisation,
and health and social care is complex.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:12.010
We are able to deliver that and have managed
very complex programmes in the past.

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.430
We are committed to that delivery, and it
is very clear from some of the evidence we

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:18.919
have so far that we are making progress, but
we know we still have more to do.

00:06:18.919 --> 00:06:21.560
Malcolm Grant: Jane, thanks very much for
that presentation.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:27.190
I think we are all impressed by how much we
can learn by employing people with learning

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:33.350
disabilities and the adjustments that we need
to make to our own processes.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:40.740
The Board is absolutely determined on as prompt
a delivery of this programme as we can get,

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:47.910
and I guess what concerns me still is, in
paragraph four, the post CTR review patients

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:52.040
who do not need by definition of the CTR to
be in hospital, but for whom a discharge plan

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.240
still cannot be put into place.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.990
We need to understand rather more what the
factors are that are inhibiting us getting

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:07.490
from the CTR to the discharge from hospital
that we seek, and to what extent is this conflict

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:13.940
of interest, vested interest, bureaucratic
delay or to what extent is there strong clinical

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:16.280
reason for the failure to discharge.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:20.800
Jane Cummings: The answer to that is that
it is mixed.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.680
You need to look at this on a case-by-case
basis.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:30.139
There are some cases where there is a lack
of agreement between the people who did the

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:34.770
Care and Treatment Review - that is, the clinicians
who came in, the commissioners from local

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:39.530
authorities and CCGs who went in, the experts
by experience who went in - and then what

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:42.920
the clinicians looking after the patient in
the hospital may have said.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:44.990
There was a difference of opinion.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:48.710
Sometimes there is a difference of opinion
with the individual patients and families;

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:53.830
they may not be completely certain about what
it is they want to see or what services they

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:55.259
want.

00:07:55.259 --> 00:08:01.550
We have also had issues with having staff
who are appropriately trained and experienced

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:04.960
who are available in the community setting
to support a discharge.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:11.270
For some people there are housing issues,
so perhaps they need to purchase a house or

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:18.250
make alterations to housing; again, that can
cause delays for the right reasons.

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:24.960
It is very individually focused, so what we
have done in paragraph four is summarised

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.240
several reasons into the things that have
the biggest impact.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:39.050
What we are trying to do is radically change
the way patients are cared for, and it is

00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:41.229
difficult taking some people with us on that.

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:43.580
Not everybody agrees that it is the right
thing to do.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:46.900
Some families do not agree that it is the
right thing to do.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:51.900
Therefore, we need to look at each individual
patient and work through with the local families,

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:56.850
with the carers and with the communities on
making sure that we can address some of those

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.410
issues.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.450
It is not straightforward to say it is one
or the other, therefore.

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:07.001
The vast majority of them are a combination
of factors, and the experience we have had

00:09:07.001 --> 00:09:11.920
so far has been to look at each of those individual
factors and to deal with each of them individually

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.870
and sort them out, and then you end up with
the result that patients can be discharged.

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:22.080
However, the fundamental issue for me is that
we can continue to do the CTRs and we can

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.070
continue to learn from them and we can continue
to increase discharges.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:30.790
The new models of care, the standards and
the commissioning framework which support

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:36.220
commissioners to commission the right services
and enable providers to provide the right

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.529
services in the right place, are the focus,
and that is what we are concentrating a lot

00:09:40.529 --> 00:09:42.440
of effort on at the moment.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:47.630
Malcolm Grant: Would it be right to summarise
that by saying what the CTR does is decide

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:52.230
whether the patient should be discharged,
and then the subsequent thing is how?

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:53.690
Jane Cummings: Yes.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:57.740
They identify if it is possible for the patient
to be discharged, if it is the right thing

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:02.070
for that patient, and then it is about looking
through what we need to do to make that happen.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:04.660
Malcolm Grant: Once the CTR has reached a
conclusion, there is a presumption that the

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:07.130
patient will be discharged and it is a question
then of how.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:11.890
Jane Cummings: Yes, and then the local teams
- the local commissioners - supported by the

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:15.960
NHS England regional teams are the ones that
are supporting that process locally.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:18.950
Malcolm Grant: Let me invite other comments
and queries.

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:27.320
Moira Gibb: I just wanted to ask whether the
Ministry of Justice was engaged not just on

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.952
an individual case when it appears that there
is an order in place, but are they engaged

00:10:30.952 --> 00:10:31.952
in the programme?

00:10:31.952 --> 00:10:32.952
Jane Cummings: Yes.

00:10:32.952 --> 00:10:33.952
Moira Gibb: That is good.

00:10:33.952 --> 00:10:43.100
Can I ask about paragraphs 16 and 17 on data
and information?

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:47.450
I was anxious that there was a data problem
when we are talking about such a small number

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:51.230
of people, and has it been resolved?

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:52.230
It says the end of April it is resolved.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:54.600
I do not really understand.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:59.520
There is obviously two different registers
or something like that.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.670
Jane Cummings: Yes, there were two different
ones.

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:07.610
The Learning Disability Census was set up
originally, which was a provider based census

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:12.970
where providers were asked to submit information
about the patients they had as inpatients.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:19.959
Of course, some of those providers had people
from out of England, so they were from other

00:11:19.959 --> 00:11:20.959
countries.

00:11:20.959 --> 00:11:25.410
The Assuring Transformation one, which is
the one that we established, was a commissioner

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:31.209
based set of data asking commissioners to
determine what patients they had in hospital

00:11:31.209 --> 00:11:32.209
and where.

00:11:32.209 --> 00:11:36.890
Hence there was a gap; that gap has now been
narrowed, and we have now transferred the

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:40.000
data collection to HSCIC.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.640
It is now on the clinical audit framework,
it is now much more regularly done and there

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.940
is better data quality.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:52.790
We still have some way to go, but there is
better data quality now in terms of what we

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:54.510
are able to report.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:57.110
John Burn: Jane, thanks.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:12:03.040
The comments on lack of provider and lack
of skills in the community are reminiscent

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:05.620
of experience in the past with paediatrics.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:10.519
One programme I was involved with managed
to get children out of hospital by allowing

00:12:10.519 --> 00:12:16.089
the staff nurses on the wards to go to their
homes and deliver drugs in their home and

00:12:16.089 --> 00:12:17.680
cross that boundary.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:23.370
Is there any scope from a commissioning point
of view to try to break down some of the silo

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:27.830
lines so that it is possible to get people
out of hospital into the community, but continue

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:31.110
to be cared for in part by people in the hospital?

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:34.050
Is that an area where we could have an influence?

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:35.050
Jane Cummings: Absolutely.

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:41.640
Health Education England is part of the Delivery
Board I Chair, and so there is a piece of

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:47.480
work about the workforce on a longer-term
basis, the skills and the necessary training.

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:51.899
However, through the fast tracks, one of the
things we want to do is look at how the workforce

00:12:51.899 --> 00:12:54.490
can be used flexibly.

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:59.680
We know that if we start to reduce the number
of inpatient beds, the staff who support those

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:04.040
inpatient beds could and possibly should provide
care in a different setting.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.680
There may be some training and education needs
around that, and they need support to do it,

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:13.230
but you are absolutely right: working flexibly
and across those traditional organisational

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:17.399
boundaries has to be the way forward, and
we have seen some of that, but we need to

00:13:17.399 --> 00:13:21.459
make that more mainstream and I would expect
the fast tracks to be a really good test site

00:13:21.459 --> 00:13:22.720
for that.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:31.410
Margaret Casely Hayford: My question is also
about paragraph 16, and it relates to the

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:41.880
way in which the data might be used to support
the cultural shift that is required with regard

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:46.850
to management, because you indicated that
some of the delays and difficulties were due

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:51.800
to management concerns.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:14:01.779
I was wondering about how effectively we could
perhaps co create a system with HSCIC to enable

00:14:01.779 --> 00:14:12.149
better use of the data, so there is systemic
improvement rather than trying to seek the

00:14:12.149 --> 00:14:17.579
improvement on a one-to-one basis of understanding
what is right for each individual.

00:14:17.579 --> 00:14:25.010
There must be learnings that are much more
deep seated within the system through an understanding

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:26.100
of the evidence.

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:32.850
Jane Cummings: We are working with HSCIC at
the moment to look at how we can use the data

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:37.480
that is collected to be shared more widely.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:41.230
In some cases, we need to be quite careful;
since they are quite small numbers, we need

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:45.010
to make sure that we are not sharing patient
identifiable information.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:51.130
However, what has been very interesting already
is demonstrating the numbers of patients and

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:56.250
the differing numbers of patients across the
country, but which are not based on anything

00:14:56.250 --> 00:15:00.519
other than geography, because there is no
other reason why we would have such diverse

00:15:00.519 --> 00:15:01.630
numbers who are inpatients.

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:02.630
Thus we are doing that and are working with
them.

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:06.250
I do not know whether Tim wants to add anything
around HSCIC?

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:12.060
Tim Kelsey: Coincidentally, we have a meeting
this week with the Chief Executive of the

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:15.750
Health and Social Care Information Centre
to make sure that we are very specifically

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:22.079
prioritising development of much more agile,
real time information resources to improve

00:15:22.079 --> 00:15:27.779
the way in which we plan these programmes,
again in the context, as Jane has mentioned,

00:15:27.779 --> 00:15:32.120
of ensuring that there is no compromising
of patient identifiability.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:36.550
Therefore, we are making process.

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:38.120
Jane Cummings: Transparency is absolutely
key.

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:43.670
It is something we are passionate about, and
being able to demonstrate that and being very

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:46.050
clear about the improvements that are being
made and the differences or variations is

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:53.940
a very powerful way of making progress.

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:58.110
Ciaran Devane: 43% of the original cohort
being in a better setting, either inpatient

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:02.100
or outpatient, probably is more encouraging
that we had last time.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:08.029
Just to pick up on one thing: where there
is failure to agree, if the review team and

00:16:08.029 --> 00:16:14.270
the family are supportive of a change of setting
I would hate to imagine that a professional

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:21.640
or a clinical view could stop that.

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:27.440
Some of the people who are used to the existing
system will have that vested interest in sticking

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:31.790
to it, whether consciously or unconsciously,
so is it about everybody has to agree, or

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:35.540
is it saying, 'We hear you, but the CTR says
this?'

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:38.410
Jane Cummings: That is a very good question.

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:44.350
Ultimately, the teams in hospitals have a
responsibility for those individual patients,

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:49.639
but we are seeing individual people shifting,
and being honest, there is something about

00:16:49.639 --> 00:16:57.019
demonstrating the art of the possible and
being able to provide inpatient clinicians

00:16:57.019 --> 00:17:00.980
with understanding that it is safe to do this.

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:05.630
There are some concerns that are raised about,
'We are not sure whether it is safe for us

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:06.630
to be able to do this.

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:07.630
These are our patients.

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:09.949
We do not want to compromise that.'

00:17:09.949 --> 00:17:14.650
Therefore, part of what we are doing is effectively
proving that it is safe and that we can move

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:19.280
people and they are receiving a better service
and better experience and are happier out

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:20.860
of hospital.

00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:26.630
We have seen some shifts in views and thoughts
as a result of some of that.

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:31.950
In addition, we do have clinicians and consultant
psychiatrists who are working with us who

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:37.170
are absolutely committed to this and are able
to give very good examples of cases where

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:43.400
people have been moved out of hospital, and
that in itself is making a difference as well.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:46.840
David Roberts: This is a dangerous question.

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:54.100
We have a lot of things to get done over the
course of the next few months to get to September,

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:58.240
which we are all committed to do.

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:04.100
If you had a magic wand and you could wave
it one or two times to change something that

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:11.929
would give you more ability to move more rapidly,
what would it be and what help could we give

00:18:11.929 --> 00:18:12.929
you?

00:18:12.929 --> 00:18:18.020
Jane Cummings: A bit of warning on that question
would have been good.

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:20.350
What would it be?

00:18:20.350 --> 00:18:28.900
Waiving some of the rules around transfer
of funding and money, making it easier to

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:29.900
flex funding.

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:36.221
It is not impossible, but it is quite complex,
so making some of that happen., Also, waving

00:18:36.221 --> 00:18:43.150
a magic wand and creating a really good range
of community providers that are in place,

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:47.320
are staffed and able to take people tomorrow
would be great.

00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:51.260
David Roberts: The latter seems to be more
about capability building.

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:58.090
The former seems to be more in the gift of
various elements, of which we would be one,

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:02.000
so is that possible to do in a short period
of time?

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:06.090
I am just thinking: is that something that
the Board could provide some encouragement

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:15.760
and some momentum behind to make it easier
to improve the lives of the people we are

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:16.760
talking about?

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:17.929
Jane Cummings: Paul might want to add to this.

00:19:17.929 --> 00:19:25.049
Through the fast track programme we will be
looking at working more collaboratively between

00:19:25.049 --> 00:19:27.580
specialised commissioning, CCG commissioning
and local authority commissioning.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:32.929
We have some good opportunities over the summer
to look at how we can implement that more

00:19:32.929 --> 00:19:36.790
quickly, so I think there is a general commitment
to that.

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:40.549
I do not know if you want to add anything
on if we could speed up those changes any

00:19:40.549 --> 00:19:41.549
quicker, but we have a plan.

00:19:41.549 --> 00:19:43.070
We need to see how it works in practice.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:46.940
Simon Stevens: I do not have a magic wand,
but I do have a team that is working on this

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:48.090
particular issue at the moment.

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:51.240
I do not think speed of getting to the solution
is going to be the problem.

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:56.330
I think once we have identified where the
money needs to move from and to, we have all

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:59.370
the flexibility available to us that we will
need.

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:04.799
The only obvious point is we can only spend
the money once, and that seems to me the slightly

00:20:04.799 --> 00:20:06.470
bigger challenge in all of this.

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:10.490
As we know, we have put aside a small amount
of money as a transformation fund - small

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:16.900
in our total context, quite big in the context
of learning disabilities - but I can give

00:20:16.900 --> 00:20:22.020
the Board total assurance that we will sort
out the money flows to enable this to happen

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:25.250
as long as we can define where the money needs
to go from and to.

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:29.010
Jane Cummings: We also have work on personal
budgets as well.

00:20:29.010 --> 00:20:38.620
Some of the pilot sites are looking at individualised
personal budgets, and from the stories we

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:43.090
hear from people who have had access to personal
budgets and the impact that has had on their

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:51.270
and their family's ability to create a service
and buy service for the family member, all

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:55.990
you have to do is listen to that story once
or twice and you know that that is a very

00:20:55.990 --> 00:20:57.370
positive way of making things move.

00:20:57.370 --> 00:21:02.180
We have that as a pilot with the IPC pilots,
and we are also looking at the other personalised

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:03.180
budget ones as well.

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:07.390
David Roberts: Maybe that is something, Chairman,
we could come back to in one of our development

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:08.390
sessions or whatever.

00:21:08.390 --> 00:21:13.200
It seems to me that being demanding of the
Executive around making those things happens

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:18.120
and personal budgets is quite important in
unlocking some of these things.

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:23.770
Simon Stevens: On that point, on NHS Change
Day part of how we spent the afternoon was

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:28.940
with families and people with learning disabilities,
and what they were telling me was about the

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:32.220
power that personal budgets had had in unlocking
service transformation.

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:38.710
I met a particular young woman called Claire
Steeples, who described to me, with her family,

00:21:38.710 --> 00:21:39.710
her experience.

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:46.679
They have a little website that sets this
out, so I am going to give them a little blast

00:21:46.679 --> 00:21:52.730
of publicity now; it is www.cpstrust.co.uk,
and there you can see pictures of Clare, who

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:57.530
had spent years in institutional settings,
now doing things like going sailing in the

00:21:57.530 --> 00:21:58.630
Solent.

00:21:58.630 --> 00:22:02.220
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Jane.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:07.470
I think all the Board had experience of this
when we were involved in the NHS Citizens

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:11.130
session in Leeds, and just getting that personal
testimony of somebody who had been able to

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:12.890
use a personal budget.

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:14.200
Jane, two things.

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:21.590
First of all, the Board is wholly seized of
the need to take this programme forward and

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:26.330
with urgency, and has been right from the
beginning, because of the difference it makes

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:30.549
to the lives of people and the clear evidence
that we have of its success.

00:22:30.549 --> 00:22:36.460
Secondly, it is complicated, but complicated
is what we do and this is complicated because

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:41.720
it is changing quite a significantly embedded
bit of culture.

00:22:41.720 --> 00:22:46.380
That is something that needs to be rolled
out and replicated amongst many other things

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:50.680
that we are doing, so we are right behind
you and we are going to keep pushing you.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:51.680
Jane Cummings: I welcome that.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:57.130
Malcolm Grant: We wish to continually revisit
the programme and measure progress.

00:22:57.130 --> 00:22:58.130
Good.

00:22:58.130 --> 00:23:03.340
If there are no other observations on Jane's
report, may I just invite Simon to make a

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:04.730
comment before we move to the next item?

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:06.040
Simon Stevens: Yes, sorry, it is not on that.

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:10.710
It is just that, characteristically, I realise
I understated the figure for the temporary

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:11.720
staffing overspend.

00:23:11.720 --> 00:23:17.169
For those in the Twittersphere, it was not
£1.4 billion of overspending on temporary

00:23:17.169 --> 00:23:19.500
staff last year, it was £1.8 billion.

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:26.090
It was £1 billion in the FT sector, £800
million in the NHS Trust sector, representing

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:30.820
an £800 million year-over-year increase in
temporary staffing expenditure.

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:33.960
So £1.8 billion is what we are up against.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:38.610
Malcolm Grant: Right, okay, we will absorb
that.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:40.470
Thank you, Simon.

